It was a cold, rainy day. The rain was pouring down in buckets. This, however, was hardly surprising—after all, such days make up 95% of the year in Riga.* On Zemitānu Boulevard, a minor traffic jam had formed once again, quickly paralyzing the entire surrounding neighborhood and leaving hundreds of people unable to get to work, school, or other destinations. For instance, the passengers of the No. 14 trolleybus, who, despite being on a completely different street, had already been forced to stand for 15 minutes on Biķernieku Street, not far from the turn onto Brīvības Street.
* Statistically speaking, this is an exaggeration; emotionally, it is the absolute truth.
The outraged passengers began to get angry: some at their fellow passengers, those in turn at the driver, the drivers at the drivers of the neighboring cars, and those, consequently, at the Riga City Council for failing to repair the roads properly, forcing them to be dug up again every single year. The leadership, meanwhile, directed its anger at their voter approval ratings, which were steadily declining by the day.**
** Then again, this happens to every government, with a few rare exceptions.
And just like that, the circle was complete. At that exact moment, down that very same Brīvības Street, a Bentley manufactured in the early 20th century sped past, turning onto the Riga bypass and heading south.
*** This story is a nod to "Good Omens" by Neil Gaiman and Terry Pratchett.
